  "Question": "A right circular cone of volume \\(A,\\) a right circular cylinder of volume \\(M,\\) and a sphere of volume \\(C\\) all have the same radius, and the common height of the cone and the cylinder is equal to the diameter of the sphere. Then",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(A-M+C=0\\)",
    "B": "\\(A+M=C\\)",
    "C": "\\(2A=M+C\\)",
    "D": "\\(A^2-M^2+C^2=0\\)",
    "E": "\\(2A+2M=3C\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "Let the common radius be \\(r>0\\). The sphere's diameter is \\(2r\\), so this is also the height of the cone and cylinder. Using one variable makes their volumes easy to compare:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nA&=\\frac13\\pi r^2(2r)=\\frac23\\pi r^3,\\\\\nM&=\\pi r^2(2r)=2\\pi r^3,\\\\\nC&=\\frac43\\pi r^3.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe cone and sphere volumes add to\n\\[\nA+C=\\left(\\frac23+\\frac43\\right)\\pi r^3\n=2\\pi r^3=M.\n\\]\nHence \\(\\boxed{A-M+C=0}\\), choice A.",
